# NEMRAS Development Rules

## Project
- This is an existing final-year healthcare project called NEMRAS.
- Do not recreate the project from scratch.
- Preserve the existing architecture unless a change is explicitly approved.
- Inspect existing code before creating new files or components.

## Flutter
- Preserve the existing NEMRAS visual identity.
- Reuse existing screens, widgets, models and navigation where appropriate.
- Do not delete existing screens without explicit approval.
- Do not create duplicate screens or duplicate app entry points.
- Keep Android, iOS, Web, Windows and macOS builds working.
- Run flutter analyze after Flutter changes.
- Run relevant Flutter tests after implementation.

## Backend
- FastAPI is the NEMRAS backend.
- Keep API contracts documented and versioned.
- Do not expose patient data without authorization.
- Do not modify the source dataset directly.
- Use the backend for healthcare business logic.
- Never put healthcare business logic only in Flutter UI.

## Firebase
- Firebase Authentication is the identity layer.
- CNIC is a NEMRAS patient identifier, not a Firebase UID.
- Firebase-generated UID must remain the identity identifier.
- Never store OTP values in Firestore.
- Temporary demo OTP is allowed only when explicitly requested.
- Production authentication must use Firebase Authentication.

## Healthcare data
- Treat NEMRAS_Dataset.csv as read-only.
- Never invent real patient medical records.
- Clearly label synthetic/demo data.
- Do not expose unnecessary patient information.
- Patient access must be authorization-controlled and auditable.

## Security
- Never commit passwords, API secrets, private keys, service-account files or real credentials.
- Do not expose raw patient phone numbers.
- Emergency/break-glass access must be explicitly authorized and audited.
- Authentication and authorization must be enforced by the backend.
- Never rely on Flutter UI restrictions as the security boundary.

## AI/ML
- Never claim a feature is AI/ML if it is only a rule or hardcoded demo.
- Clearly distinguish:
  - rule-based logic
  - trained ML models
  - RAG
  - LLM responses
  - demo/mock behaviour
- Every trained model must have reproducible evaluation.
- AI responses must remain grounded in approved data sources.

## UI/UX
- NEMRAS must have a professional enterprise healthcare interface.
- Maintain consistent typography, spacing, colors, cards, tables, navigation and status indicators.
- Support responsive layouts.
- Patient/mobile UI and hospital/desktop UI may use different layout patterns while sharing the same design language.
- Do not randomly redesign existing screens.
- UI references supplied by the project owner should be treated as design direction.

## Architecture
- Prefer reusable packages/components over duplicated code.
- Keep patient, paramedic, hospital, ministry and portfolio experiences logically separated.
- Keep shared models, API clients and design components reusable.
- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.

## Git
- Never commit or push changes unless explicitly requested.
- Before major changes, inspect git status.
- Do not overwrite unrelated user changes.
- Keep changes focused on the requested task.

## Agent workflow
- Work incrementally.
- Before large changes, explain the files that will be changed.
- Implement only the requested phase/module.
- After implementation, run appropriate tests/analyzer.
- Report:
  1. files changed
  2. features implemented
  3. tests run
  4. errors fixed
  5. remaining issues
- Do not silently implement future phases.
